y about like  how  there's kind of a real strategic art  which i don't think i'm a master of and  i think you sort of have to  think about day by day of  saying yes to everything initially  and then unlearning that skill at a  certain point um like saying yes to  everything in a way that's going to help  you meet people and expose yourself to  new ideas and whatever but then  realizing that you have to unlearn that  skill in order to narrow your focus on  any given opportunity or um experience  to like write something that's a bit  more substantial i've still not figured  that out anytime you know me either i  know i i've gotten far enough to know  that's what one must do  [Laughter]  yeah it's uh it's a start we're still  we're all still trying to figure it out  yeah  um jason how about you any advice for  people who are actually trying to get  worse as ready yeah  like yeah cause i reckon um you know for  people that have purely just writing  jobs in the industry um they are  like you know that kind of thing is a  little bit like you know a unicorn or  whatever uh like you know there are  those people but um so many of the  people that i know that do writing and  games also have other skills in games  and kind of like you know you might be a  producer and a writer or you might be a  programmer and a writer or you might be  you know something else in a writer and  kind of like  um  having uh like yeah that this is very  yeah a very practical thing but like  having more that you can offer as  opposed to like i'm just like i'm just  into writing and that's the only thing  that i like and i'm really good at it  and i don't do anything else  um  it's like  like i think it's just way more likely  if you've got a diversity of skills that  somebody's gonna go like oh yeah you  know we can bring this person on they  can be doing this when  when we need writing like they can be  doing writing they can do this when we  don't need writing because there's  often there are like